### What is the Minimum Subset Sum Difference Problem?

Given a set of positive numbers, partition them into two subsets so that the absolute difference of their sums is minimized. Return that minimum difference. This is a classic optimization built on the Subset Sum reachability pattern.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh needs to divide a batch of payouts between two settlement tellers as evenly as possible to balance their workloads.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MinSubsetSumDiff {
    public static int minDifference(int[] nums) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int n : nums) total += n;
        int half = total / 2;

        boolean[] dp = new boolean[half + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int num : nums) {
            for (int t = half; t >= num; t--) {
                if (dp[t - num]) dp[t] = true;
            }
        }

        // Find largest reachable sum <= half
        for (int s = half; s >= 0; s--) {
            if (dp[s]) {
                return total - 2 * s;
            }
        }
        return total;  // should not reach here
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] payouts = {1000, 2000, 3000, 4000};
        System.out.println("Min difference: " + minDifference(payouts));
        // Total = 10000, half = 5000
        // Reachable: {1000+4000=5000} -> diff = 10000 - 2*5000 = 0
    }
}
```

```text
Min difference: 0
```

### Why Does This Reduce to Subset Sum?

When you partition numbers into two subsets, one subset sums to S1 and the other to total - S1. The difference is |total - 2*S1|. To minimize this, you want S1 as close to total/2 as possible. Finding the largest reachable S1 at or below total/2 is exactly a Subset Sum problem with target = total/2.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class DifferenceFormula {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 10000;
        int s1 = 5000;  // best reachable sum <= half
        int s2 = total - s1;  // 5000
        int diff = Math.abs(s2 - s1);  // 0
        // Equivalently: diff = total - 2 * s1 = 10000 - 10000 = 0
        System.out.println("S1=" + s1 + ", S2=" + s2 + ", diff=" + diff);
    }
}
```

```text
S1=5000, S2=5000, diff=0
```

### How Does the Algorithm Work Step by Step?

First compute the total and set the DP target to total/2. Then run the standard Subset Sum boolean DP with high-to-low sweep. Finally, scan the DP array downward from total/2 to find the largest reachable sum.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MinDiffTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {1, 6, 11, 5};
        int total = 23;
        int half = 11;

        boolean[] dp = new boolean[half + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int num : nums) {
            System.out.println("Processing: " + num);
            for (int t = half; t >= num; t--) {
                if (dp[t - num] && !dp[t]) {
                    dp[t] = true;
                    System.out.println("  dp[" + t + "] = true");
                }
            }
        }

        for (int s = half; s >= 0; s--) {
            if (dp[s]) {
                System.out.println("Best S1 = " + s + ", diff = " + (total - 2 * s));
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Processing: 1
  dp[1] = true
Processing: 6
  dp[7] = true
  dp[6] = true
Processing: 11
  dp[11] = true
Processing: 5
  dp[11] already true
Best S1 = 11, diff = 1
```

### How Is This Different from Equal Partition?

| Aspect | Equal Partition | Minimum Subset Sum Difference |
|---|---|---|
| Question | Can we split into two EQUAL halves? | What is the SMALLEST difference? |
| Answer type | Boolean (yes/no) | Integer (the minimum difference) |
| Condition | Only possible if total is even | Always has an answer |
| Reduction | Subset Sum where target = total/2 exactly | Subset Sum where target = total/2, then scan |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class EqualVsMinDiff {
    // Equal Partition: return dp[total/2] (boolean)
    // Min Diff: scan dp from total/2 downward, return total - 2 * best
}
```

### What Are the Edge Cases?

Edge cases include: a single element (difference equals that element), two elements (difference equals their absolute difference), and all elements equal (difference is 0 if count is even, one element's value if count is odd).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MinDiffEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Single element
        System.out.println(MinSubsetSumDiff.minDifference(new int[]{5000}));  // 5000

        // Two elements
        System.out.println(MinSubsetSumDiff.minDifference(new int[]{3000, 7000}));  // 4000

        // All equal, even count
        System.out.println(MinSubsetSumDiff.minDifference(new int[]{1000, 1000, 1000, 1000}));  // 0

        // All equal, odd count
        System.out.println(MinSubsetSumDiff.minDifference(new int[]{1000, 1000, 1000}));  // 1000
    }
}
```

```text
5000
4000
0
1000
```

### What About Reconstructing the Actual Partition?

The 1D boolean array tells us the minimum difference but not which numbers go into which subset. To reconstruct, use the 2D DP table and trace back from the best sum.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class MinDiffReconstruct {
    public static List<Integer> findSubset(int[] nums, int target) {
        int n = nums.length;
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n + 1][target + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = true;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int t = 1; t <= target; t++) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
                if (t >= nums[i - 1]) dp[i][t] = dp[i][t] || dp[i - 1][t - nums[i - 1]];
            }
        }
        // Trace back
        List<Integer> subset = new ArrayList<>();
        int t = target;
        for (int i = n; i > 0 && t > 0; i--) {
            if (!dp[i - 1][t]) {  // nums[i-1] was used
                subset.add(nums[i - 1]);
                t -= nums[i - 1];
            }
        }
        return subset;
    }
}
```

### What About Time and Space Complexity?

The time complexity is O(N * total/2) and the space is O(total/2) with the 1D array. If the total is very large, this becomes impractical. For extremely large totals, approximation algorithms or meet-in-the-middle approaches are used.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MinDiffComplexity {
    // Time: O(N * total/2)
    // Space: O(total/2) with 1D array, O(N * total/2) with 2D
    // If total is very large (e.g., 10^9), DP is impractical
    // Meet-in-the-middle: O(2^(N/2)) for small N, large values
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Equal Partition: is the minimum difference zero? Only when total is even and dp[total/2] is true.
- Count the number of ways to partition with minimum difference.
- Handle negative numbers: shift all values to make them positive, adjust the target accordingly.
- Extend to three-way partition: this becomes harder and requires 2D DP over two target sums.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MinDiffFollowUps {
    // Equal Partition: minDifference(nums) == 0 when total is even
    // Three-way: dp[t1][t2] tracking two subset sums simultaneously
}
```
